# Zenodo and GitHub read this file to build the citation record for a release.
# Update `version` and `date-released` on every tagged release.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this code, please cite it using the metadata below.
title: >-
  Linearity Is a Regime, Not a Law: reproducibility code for
  frequency degrees of freedom as native nonlinearity primitives
  for optical computing
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Özdemir
    given-names: Muhammed Can
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4002-7967'
    affiliation: Independent researcher
    email: info@mucan.dev
    city: Istanbul
    country: TR
version: 1.0.0
date-released: '2026-07-31'
license: MIT
repository-code: 'https://github.com/mucan54/linearity-is-a-regime'
url: 'https://github.com/mucan54/linearity-is-a-regime'
abstract: >-
  Numerical models behind the paper "Linearity Is a Regime, Not a Law".
  The code contains a self-consistent steady-state coupled-mode model of a
  thin-film lithium niobate microring operated as a self-action activation;
  component- and core-level energy budgets for a 256x256 optical tensor core,
  including the activation bank's own optical drive charged at the ring's
  linewidth rather than the system symbol rate; a roofline analysis of
  autoregressive decode under batching; a cascaded-Lorentzian softmax study;
  and depth-resolved noise-injection studies on character-level transformers
  covering independent, correlated common-mode and complex-phase perturbations.
  All results are idealized simulations; no device was fabricated or measured.
keywords:
  - optical computing
  - photonic accelerator
  - optical nonlinearity
  - microring resonator
  - thin-film lithium niobate
  - Kerr self-action
  - frequency comb
  - transformer
  - energy efficiency
  - reproducibility
